      "math_academy_id": "q-112763",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/699dc5f983ed428e6fa6adcb3c6f32a28e24e7022784a88b425835614760c34e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(2x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(2x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/01de458656dbcc6b29236cf4db76c2dd1a1ee8f503c269759b25e13f7756def0.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/e496df922bcae419f75857f7774212299ea5045c524c9b5dd906022fe5a5e5eb.png)",
